open_image fails for transient/recoverable reasons that are NOT 'this photo has no face': HEIC/HEIF when the Microsoft HEIF Image Extension is not yet installed (convert_heic_to_image hard-fails), RAW needing a codec not yet installed, a file briefly locked by another process, or a flaky network/removable drive. The v1.5.401 up-front-mark rationale was specifically for faceless photos, not for 'could not even decode'. Because no command ever resets faces_scanned to 0, these photos are excluded from every future scan and the user has no way to retry them.

backfill_dates fills missing date_taken using EXIF, else file created/modified time, with no placeholder filtering and no oldest-wins logic:

```
let date = crate::exif_reader::read_exif(path).ok().and_then(|e| e.date_taken)
    .or_else(|| {
        std::fs::metadata(path).ok().and_then(|m| {
            m.created().or_else(|_| m.modified()).ok().map(|t| {
                let dt: chrono::DateTime<chrono::Local> = t.into();
                dt.format("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S").to_string()
            })
        })
    });
```

This directly contradicts the scanner's deliberate v1.5.266 decision (scanner.rs lines ~310-320) to DROP mtime/ctime as a date source because 'mtime is a lie for date taken' (it's usually the date the file was dragged onto the PC), leaving such photos date_taken=NULL on purpose. Running this user-facing backfill writes those import-time timestamps as capture dates for every EXIF-less photo (Facebook/WhatsApp exports, screenshots), which then drives the Newest/Oldest sort, the timeline, and the calendar to show wrong dates — exactly the bug v1.5.266 was meant to prevent.
